// c-select-seq-01: a bottom or undecidable select window leaves the held
// arm awake but never updated; whatever lands inside the arm then is lost.
//
// command: graphix --no-cache design/review-2026-10-05/repro/c-select-seq-01.gx
//          (the same with --no-fusion, GRAPHIX_PAR=force, a cold or warm image)
//
// `sel` is null while n is 2..3 (100 ms), so `sel$` is bottom and
// Select::update returns early (graphix-compiler/src/node/select.rs:920).
// The selected arm is not evaluated and not slept. The timer's ticks in
// that window reach no one. Timer only re-arms when it sees its own tick
// in ctx.event.variables (stdlib/graphix-package-sys/src/time.rs), so it
// never fires again. When sel is 1 again, the same arm is selected and
// awake, and its timer is dead for good.
//
// expected: counting resumes once sel is 1 again (from 1 if the window
//   pauses the arm, onward from ~9 if it keeps running). Control: with
//   `2 => 1` in place of `2 => null` it prints 1 2 3 4 4 5 6 ... 28.
// observed (every mode): 1 2 3 4 4, then nothing until the exit at n = 12.
//
// Same window, other victims (each probe run both ways, --no-fusion and not):
//
// 1. A seq waiting on an async step wedges. Replace the last line with
//      let go = select n { 1 | 8 => n, _ => never() };
//      select sel$ { _ => seq go { let r = sys::time::after_idle(duration:80.ms, go); r * 10 } }
//    expected: 10 10 80 (what `2 => 1` prints). observed: nothing. Run 1's
//    reply lands in the window, so the machine stays busy and drops run 2.
//
// 2. An arm-local write that lands in the window is lost, and the engines
//    disagree about it:
//      let clock = sys::time::timer(duration:50.ms, true);
//      let n = 0;
//      n <- clock ~ n + 1;
//      let click = select n { 2 | 6 => n, _ => never() };
//      let sel: [i64, null] = 5;
//      sel <- select n { 2 => null, 4 => 7, _ => never() };
//      sys::exit(select n { 8 => 0, _ => never() });
//      select sel$ { k => { let clicks = 0; clicks <- click ~ clicks + 1; (clicks * 100, k) } }
//    The first click's `clicks <- 1` lands in the window's first cycle.
//    expected: (0, 5) (100, 7) (200, 7)
//    observed, node-walk: (0, 5) (0, 7) (100, 7). `clicks * 100` rides its
//      pre-window resident while the store holds 1, and `clicks + 1` rides
//      1, so the second click is lost too.
//    observed, JIT: (0, 5) (100, 7) (100, 7). The kernel computes from the
//      store; the second click is still lost.
//    The fuzzer sees it: driven by fuzz inputs instead of the clock
//    (click = skip(#n: 1, in1), sel <- skip(#n: 1, in0), sel <- click ~ null,
//    schedule in1=i64:1 then in0=i64:7), graphix-fuzz check reports
//    DIVERGENCE, interp [0, 7] vs jit [100, 7].
//
// An undecidable guard window (a consulted guard standing bottom,
// ChainOut::Undet, select.rs:973) freezes the timer the same way. A lambda
// whose argument is bottom keeps its body running, and its timer keeps
// counting.
let clock = sys::time::timer(duration:50.ms, true);
let n = 0;
n <- clock ~ n + 1;
let sel: [i64, null] = 1;
sel <- select n { 2 => null, 4 => 1, _ => never() };
sys::exit(select n { 12 => 0, _ => never() });
select sel$ { _ => count(sys::time::timer(duration:20.ms, true)) }
